# minimum required Qore version
%requires qore >= 1.14

# requires the SqlUtil module
%requires SqlUtil >= 1.9

# don't use "$" signs for variables and class members, assume local variable scope
%modern

# require type definitions everywhere

# enable all warnings

module XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase {
    version = "0.2";
    desc = "user module for working with Firebird DBs using xDBC drivers";
    author = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
}

/** @mainpage XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section xdbcfirebirdsqlutilbaseintro XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase Module Introduction

    The %XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase module provides a high level API for working with Firebird database objects through
    \c xdbc and \c jdbc drivers in %Qore.

    Usually this module is loaded on demand from the SqlUtil module, however to use this module
    directly, use \c "%requires XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase" in your code.

    All the public symbols in the module are defined in the XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase namespace

    @note
    - This module requires the <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/module-xdbc">xdbc</a> or
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/module-jni">jni</a> binary modules
      for communication with Firebird databases plus the Firebird client library and the Firebird XDBC driver or the
      Firebird JDBC drivers
    - This module supports basic DDL / schema management for Firebird DBs and basic functionality for DML as well

    @section firebird_schema_management Schema Management on Firebird

    @subsection xdbcfirebird_type_mapping Type Mapping

    Column types are mapped from %Qore types as follows:

    <b>XdbcFirebird Column Type Mappings</b>
    |!%Qore Type Name|!Firebird Type Used
    |\c int|\c bigint
    |\c float|<tt>double precision</tt>
    |\c number|\c numeric
    |\c string|\c varchar
    |\c date|<tt>timestamp</tt>
    |\c binary|\c blob
    |\c bool|\c boolean
    |@ref SqlUtil::BLOB|\c blob
    |@ref SqlUtil::CHAR|\c char
    |@ref SqlUtil::CLOB|<tt>blob sub_type text</tt>

    @note An unqualified \c NUMERIC or \c DECIMAL column is \c NUMERIC(9,0) in Firebird; numeric columns declared
    without a size therefore use the widest int64-backed precision (\c 18) instead, and a scale must be given
    explicitly to store decimal values

    @subsection xdbcfirebird_identifiers Identifiers

    Names of tables, columns, indexes, constraints, sequences, views, triggers, savepoints and aliases are given to
    Firebird as identifiers that can never change the SQL that they are part of (see @ref sql_identifiers):
    - a name that Firebird reads as one unquoted identifier (an ASCII letter, then ASCII letters, digits, underscores
      or dollar signs) is given as it is, and Firebird stores it in upper case, as before
    - a reserved word is given in double quotes in the case given, as before
    - any other name, such as one with spaces, non-ASCII letters, quotes or other punctuation, is given in upper case
      in double quotes, with each double quote doubled, so that it names the object that an unquoted name of the same
      letters would: SqlUtil looks up names in upper case on Firebird and gives the names that it reads from the
      database in lower case, so a table created with a column \c "order date" has the column \c "ORDER DATE",
      described as \c "order date"
    - an alias (@ref SqlUtil::cop_as() "cop_as()") is given in double quotes in the case given, so the key of the
      result is the alias, as before
    - an empty name or a name with a NUL character cannot be an identifier and is refused with \c IDENTIFIER-ERROR

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
# create table orders (id bigint not null, "ORDER DATE" timestamp)
string sql = table.getCreateTableSql();
    @endcode

    @subsection xdbcfirebird_fk_actions Foreign Constraint Referential Actions

    Firebird supports the \c on_delete and \c on_update
    @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::ForeignConstraintOptions "foreign constraint options" (see
    @ref fk_actions) as follows:

    <b>Firebird Referential Actions</b>
    |!Action|!Firebird SQL|!Described as
    |NOTHING or @ref SqlUtil::FK_NO_ACTION|no clause; the database default, which refuses the delete or update of a \
      referenced row|NOTHING
    |@ref SqlUtil::FK_CASCADE|<tt>on delete cascade</tt>, <tt>on update cascade</tt>|@ref SqlUtil::FK_CASCADE
    |@ref SqlUtil::FK_SET_NULL|<tt>on delete set null</tt>, <tt>on update set null</tt>|@ref SqlUtil::FK_SET_NULL
    |@ref SqlUtil::FK_SET_DEFAULT|<tt>on delete set default</tt>, <tt>on update set default</tt>|\
      @ref SqlUtil::FK_SET_DEFAULT
    |@ref SqlUtil::FK_RESTRICT|refused with \c FOREIGN-CONSTRAINT-ERROR: Firebird has no \c RESTRICT clause, and \
      its default already refuses the delete or update of a referenced row|-

    Firebird records \c RESTRICT in its catalog for an action that is not given and \c NO \c ACTION for an action
    given as \c NO \c ACTION; both are described as NOTHING, the database default, so that a foreign constraint
    created without an action is equal to a template foreign constraint without an action, and schema alignment
    proposes no SQL for it.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
# alter table order_lines add constraint fk_order_lines_order foreign key (order_id) references orders (id)
#   on delete cascade
string sql;
table.addForeignConstraint("fk_order_lines_order", "order_id", "orders", "id", {"on_delete": FK_CASCADE}, \sql);
    @endcode

    @subsection xdbcfirebird_sequences Sequences

    A Firebird sequence has a starting value and an increment, which Firebird keeps from Firebird 3 on, and no
    minimum, maximum, cycle option or cache:
    - a sequence is created with <tt>start with</tt> and <tt>increment by</tt> when they are not the defaults (1)
    - schema alignment changes the increment of an existing sequence with <tt>alter sequence ... increment by</tt>,
      which keeps its current value; the starting value of an existing sequence is never changed, as that would
      restart it
    - a sequence description with a minimum, a maximum (\c max or \c end), the cycle option or a cache is refused
      with \c SEQUENCE-ERROR when the sequence is created or aligned; a cache of \c 0 and the cycle option
      @ref False "False" are as Firebird sequences are, and are accepted

    @note the first value of a sequence created with a starting value is the starting value from Firebird 4 on, and
    the starting value plus the increment on Firebird 3

    @section xdbcfirebird_relnotes Release Notes

    @subsection xdbcfirebirdsqlutilbasev_0_2 XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase Module v0.2
    - added support for the \c on_delete and \c on_update referential actions of foreign constraints; \c RESTRICT,
      which Firebird has no clause for, is refused with \c FOREIGN-CONSTRAINT-ERROR (see
      @ref xdbcfirebird_fk_actions)
    - fixed describing the foreign constraints of a table: none were described, so schema alignment added the
      foreign constraints of a template to a table that had them already; they are now described from the database,
      with their referential actions
    - fixed creating a sequence with the starting value and the increment of its description, which were not given,
      so every sequence was created with the defaults; schema alignment now changes the increment of an existing
      sequence, keeping its current value, and a description with a minimum, a maximum, the cycle option or a cache,
      which Firebird sequences do not have, is refused with \c SEQUENCE-ERROR (see @ref xdbcfirebird_sequences)
    - fixed listing the sequences of the database, which gave none
    - fixed SQL injection through names: the names of tables, columns, indexes, constraints, sequences, views,
      triggers and savepoints, and aliases, were given in SQL as they are, so a name with a double quote, a semicolon
      or other punctuation, as a name from a file or a data provider can have, could change the SQL statement; a name
      that is not an ordinary identifier is now quoted and escaped, an ordinary name or a reserved word is given as
      before, and a name that cannot be an identifier is refused with \c IDENTIFIER-ERROR (see
      @ref xdbcfirebird_identifiers)
    - fixed a unique constraint naming its columns as they are, so that a column whose name is a reserved word was
      not quoted
    - fixed renaming an index, as table alignment does for an index with the definition of the template's index
      under another name: the SQL that dropped the index and created it under the new name was two statements in one
      string; Firebird cannot rename an index, so such an index is now taken for the template's index and kept under
      its name in the database, without rebuilding it, and is dropped by that name (see
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractIndex::supportsRename() "AbstractIndex::supportsRename()")
    - fixed table alignment of a unique index with other columns, which dropped a unique constraint of the same
      name, dropped the index, created it and added the constraint again: the index is now dropped once and created
      once, and only the foreign constraints that reference it are dropped and added again around it
    - Firebird cannot rename a constraint: table alignment dropped a foreign constraint with the definition of the
      template's under another name and added it again under the new name; a unique or foreign constraint with the
      definition of the template's under another name is now taken for the template's and kept under its name in the
      database, so that the next alignment needs no SQL either (see
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractConstraint::supportsRename() "AbstractConstraint::supportsRename()")
    - renaming a trigger raised \c UNSUPPORTED: Firebird cannot rename a trigger, so it is dropped and created under
      the new name
    - fixed a \c cast data provider expression with a size, or with a precision and a scale: the size was ignored
      and the precision was given as the scale, so that the cast failed or changed the value
    - fixed describing the indexes of a table: an index created with \c CREATE \c INDEX was never described, only
      the indexes of unique and foreign constraints were, so schema alignment created an index again that the table
      had already
    - fixed describing the unique constraints of a table: none were described, so every schema alignment dropped the
      index of each unique constraint and added the constraint again
    - fixed describing the size of a \c CHAR or \c VARCHAR column in a database with a character set of more than
      one byte per character, such as \c UTF8: its size in bytes was described, so every schema alignment changed
      the type of the column again
    - fixed finding a table or a sequence named with a reserved word, such as \c order: it was looked up in upper
      case, but Firebird keeps such a name in the case that it is given in, so the table or sequence was never found
    - fixed reading a view, which was never found; the names of views are now listed in lower case, as the names of
      tables and sequences are; before, they were listed in upper case

    @subsection xdbcfirebirdsqlutilbasev_0_1 XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase Module v0.1
    - initial release
*/

# returns a name as an SQL identifier for Firebird: as given if Firebird reads it as one unquoted identifier (an ASCII
# letter, then ASCII letters, digits, underscores or dollar signs), so that Firebird folds it to upper case as for any
# unquoted name, as before; a reserved word in double quotes in the case given, as before; any other name in upper
# case in double quotes, with each double quote doubled, so that it names the object that an unquoted name of the same
# letters would, and can never change the SQL that it is part of; the jni and odbc drivers read no placeholder,
# comment or escape in double-quoted text; firebird_get_catalog_name() gives the name that Firebird keeps for it
string sub firebird_get_sql_identifier(string name) {
    if (name =~ /\A[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_$]*\z/) {
        return XdbcFirebirdDatabase::XdbcFirebirdReservedWords{name.lwr()} ? ("\"" + name + "\"") : name;
    }
    return AbstractSqlUtilBase::getDelimitedIdentifier(name.upr(), "\"", "\"", "Firebird");
}

# returns the name that Firebird keeps in its catalog for an object named in SQL with firebird_get_sql_identifier():
# a reserved word in the case given, as it is given in double quotes in that case, and any other name in upper case,
# as Firebird folds an unquoted name to upper case and any other name is given in upper case in double quotes
string sub firebird_get_catalog_name(string name) {
    if (name =~ /\A[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_$]*\z/ && XdbcFirebirdDatabase::XdbcFirebirdReservedWords{name.lwr()}) {
        return name;
    }
    return name.upr();
}

#! the XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase namespace contains all the objects in the XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase module
public namespace XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase {
    #! returns a XdbcFirebirdTable object corresponding to the arguments
    public XdbcFirebirdTable sub get_table(AbstractDatasource nds, string nname, *hash<auto> opts) {
        return new XdbcFirebirdTable(nds, nname, opts);
    }

    #! returns a XdbcFirebirdDatabase object corresponding to the arguments
    public XdbcFirebirdDatabase sub get_database(AbstractDatasource nds, *hash<auto> opts) {
        return new XdbcFirebirdDatabase(nds, opts);
    }

    #! XdbcFirebird savepoint helper
    public class XdbcFirebirdSavepointHelper inherits AbstractSavepointHelper {
        #! Creates the object
        constructor(AbstractDatasource ds, *string savepoint) : AbstractSavepointHelper(ds, savepoint) {
        }

        #! Creates the savepoint
        private createSavepointImpl() {
            ds.exec("savepoint " + firebird_get_sql_identifier(savepoint));
        }

        #! Rolls back to the savepoint
        private rollbackImpl() {
            ds.exec("rollback to " + firebird_get_sql_identifier(savepoint));
        }

        #! Deletes the savepoint
        private deleteSavepointImpl() {
            ds.exec("release savepoint " + firebird_get_sql_identifier(savepoint));
        }
    }

    #! represents a Firebird-specific index
    public class XdbcFirebirdIndex inherits SqlUtil::AbstractIndex {
        #! creates the object from the arguments
        constructor(string name, bool unique, hash<auto> cols) : AbstractIndex(name, unique, cols) {
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to create the index in the database
        string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            # an index "column" that is not a column of the table is an SQL expression (see AbstractTable::addIndex())
            return sprintf("create %sindex %s on %s (%s)", unique ? "unique " : "", firebird_get_sql_identifier(name),
                table_name, (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, columns.getSqlColumnNameIterator()));
        }

        #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current index, @ref False "False" if not
        private bool equalImpl(AbstractIndex ix) {
            return True;
        }

        #! returns no SQL: Firebird cannot rename an index
        /** An index is never dropped and created again under another name; see @ref supportsRename()
        */
        softlist<string> getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            return ();
        }

        #! returns @ref False "False": Firebird cannot rename an index
        /** Table alignment takes an index with the definition of a template index under another name for the
            template's index, and keeps it under its name in the database, which the SQL that drops it uses (see
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractIndex::supportsRename() "AbstractIndex::supportsRename()" and
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractIndex::getDbName() "AbstractIndex::getDbName()")

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2
        */
        bool supportsRename() {
            return False;
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to drop the index from the table, by its name in the database
        string getDropSql(string table_name) {
            return sprintf("drop index %s", firebird_get_sql_identifier(getDbName()));
        }
    }

    #! represents a Firebird-specific foreign constraint
    public class XdbcFirebirdForeignConstraint inherits SqlUtil::AbstractForeignConstraint {
        #! creates the constraint from the arguments
        /** @param n the name of the constraint
            @param c the columns of the constraint
            @param t the target table and columns
            @param on_delete the \c ON \c DELETE referential action (see @ref xdbcfirebird_fk_actions), or NOTHING for
                the database default
            @param on_update the \c ON \c UPDATE referential action (see @ref xdbcfirebird_fk_actions), or NOTHING for
                the database default

            @throw FOREIGN-CONSTRAINT-ERROR an invalid referential action, or @ref SqlUtil::FK_RESTRICT, which
            Firebird does not support

            @note \a on_delete and \a on_update are available since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2
        */
        constructor(string n, Columns c, ForeignConstraintTarget t, *string on_delete, *string on_update)
                : AbstractForeignConstraint(n, c, t, False, False, on_delete, on_update) {
            checkActions();
        }

        #! maps an \c rdb$delete_rule or \c rdb$update_rule value of \c rdb$ref_constraints to a referential action
        /** Firebird records \c RESTRICT for a foreign constraint created without an action and \c NO \c ACTION for
            one created with \c NO \c ACTION; both are the database default, which checks the constraint immediately,
            and are described as NOTHING, so that a foreign constraint of a template without an action is equal to
            the foreign constraint in the database (see @ref xdbcfirebird_fk_actions)

            @param rule the value of \c rdb$delete_rule or \c rdb$update_rule

            @return the referential action (see @ref fk_actions), or NOTHING for the database
            default

            @throw FOREIGN-CONSTRAINT-ERROR the value is not a referential action

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2
        */
        static *string actionFromCatalog(*string rule) {
            if (!rule.val()) {
                return;
            }
            string action = trim(rule);
            if (action.upr() == FK_RESTRICT) {
                return;
            }
            return normalize_fk_action(action, "rdb$ref_constraints rule");
        }

        # Firebird has no RESTRICT clause: its default already refuses the delete or update of a referenced row
        private checkActions() {
            foreach hash<auto> i in ({"on_delete": on_delete, "on_update": on_update}.pairIterator()) {
                if (i.value == FK_RESTRICT) {
                    throw "FOREIGN-CONSTRAINT-ERROR", sprintf("foreign constraint %y: Firebird does not support the %s "
                        "referential action %y; leave the action out for the database default, which refuses the "
                        "%s of a referenced row", name, i.key, FK_RESTRICT, i.key == "on_delete" ? "delete" : "update");
                }
            }
        }

        string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            return getCreateSql(name, table_name, opt);
        }

        #! returns no SQL: Firebird cannot rename a constraint
        /** A foreign constraint is never dropped and added again under another name; see @ref supportsRename()
        */
        softlist getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            return ();
        }

        #! returns @ref False "False": Firebird cannot rename a constraint
        /** Table alignment takes a foreign constraint with the definition of the template's under another name for
            the template's, and keeps it under its name in the database; see
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractConstraint::supportsRename() "AbstractConstraint::supportsRename()"

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2
        */
        bool supportsRename() {
            return False;
        }

        string getCreateSql(string name, string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            return sprintf("alter table %s ", table_name) + getAddSql(name, table_name, opt);
        }

        #! returns the \c ADD \c CONSTRAINT clause that adds the constraint, with its referential actions
        /** @throw FOREIGN-CONSTRAINT-ERROR a referential action is @ref SqlUtil::FK_RESTRICT, which Firebird does not
            support

            @note the referential actions are added since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2
        */
        string getAddSql(string name, string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            # the actions are public members, so they are checked again here
            checkActions();
            return sprintf("add constraint %s foreign key (%s) references %s (%s)%s", firebird_get_sql_identifier(name),
                (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()), firebird_get_sql_identifier(target.table),
                (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, target.columns.getSqlColumnNameIterator()), getActionsSql());
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to drop the foreign constraint from the database
        string getDropSql(string table_name) {
            return sprintf("alter table %s drop constraint %s", table_name, firebird_get_sql_identifier(name));
        }
    }

    #! represents a XdbcFirebird-specific column
    public class XdbcFirebirdColumn inherits SqlUtil::AbstractColumn {
        #! empty constructor for subclasses
        private constructor() {
        }

        #! Creates the column
        constructor(string name, string native_type, *string qore_type, int size, bool nullable,
                *string default_value, *string comment, *int scale)
                : AbstractColumn(name, native_type, qore_type, size, nullable, default_value, comment, scale) {
        }

        #! returns a list of sql strings that can be used to add the column to an existing table
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
list<string> l = col.getAddColumnSql(t);
            @endcode

            @param t the AbstractTable object to modify
         */
        softlist<string> getAddColumnSql(AbstractTable t) {
            return sprintf("alter table %s add %s", t.getSqlName(), AbstractColumn::getCreateSql(t));
        }

        #! returns a list of sql strings that can be used to modify the column to the new definition
        /** if the column definitions are identical then an empty list is returned

            @code{.py}
list<string> l = col.getModifySql(t, newcol);
            @endcode

            The column names are assumed to be equal.

            @param t the AbstractTable object to modify
            @param col the new column definition
            @param opt column modification options (none are supported in this class)

            @return a list of sql strings that can be used to modify the column to the new definition; if the column
            definitions are identical then an empty list is returned
         */
        softlist<string> getModifySqlImpl(AbstractTable t, AbstractColumn col, *hash<auto> opt) {
            list<string> l = ();
            if (nullable != col.nullable) {
                l += sprintf("alter table %s alter %s set %snull", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name,
                    col.nullable ? "" : "not ");
            }
            if (native_type != col.native_type || size != col.size || (scale ?? 0) != (col.scale ?? 0)) {
                # the target column's type must be used here; using the current column's type would emit a no-op
                l += sprintf("alter table %s alter %s type %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, col.getNativeTypeString());
            }
            if (def_val != col.def_val) {
                l += sprintf("alter table %s alter %s %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name,
                    col.def_val ? ("set default " + col.def_val) : "drop default");
            }
            return l;
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to rename the column
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
string str = col.getRenameSql(t, "new_name");
            @endcode

            @param t the AbstractTable object to modify
            @param new_name the new name for the column

            @return a string that can be used to rename the column
        */
        string getRenameSql(AbstractTable t, string new_name) {
            return sprintf("alter table %s alter %s to %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, getDdlName(new_name));
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to drop the column from the table
        string getDropSql(string table_name) {
            return sprintf("alter table %s drop %s", table_name, ddl_name);
        }

        #! returns the column name for DDL, as XdbcFirebirdTable::getSqlIdentifier() returns it
        /** the name returned here will be used when executing DDL

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2 a name that is not an ordinary name is quoted in upper case and escaped;
            before, only a reserved word was quoted
        */
        string getDdlName(string name) {
            return firebird_get_sql_identifier(name);
        }

        #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current object, @ref False "False" if not
        private bool equalImpl(AbstractColumn c) {
            return True;
        }
    }

    public class XdbcFirebirdUniqueConstraint inherits SqlUtil::AbstractUniqueConstraint {
        constructor(string name, hash<auto> cols) : AbstractUniqueConstraint(name, cols) {
        }

        #! returns the XdbcFirebirdColumn value of the given key if it exists, otherwise throws a \c KEY-ERROR exception
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
auto v = c.name;
            @endcode

            @param k the name of the key to access

            @return the value of the given key in the contained hash if it exists

            @throw KEY-ERROR the given key does not exist in the contained hash

            @note this method is called automatically when an unknown or inaccessible member name is accessed from
            outside the class

            @see @ref memberGate_methods
        */
        XdbcFirebirdColumn memberGate(string k) {
            if (!h.hasKey(k)) {
                throw "KEY-ERROR", sprintf("cannot access unknown %s %y; expecting one of: %y", getElementName(), k,
                    h.keys());
            }
            return h{k};
        }

        string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opts) {
            return getCreateSql(name, table_name, opts);
        }

        #! returns no SQL: Firebird cannot rename a constraint
        /** A unique constraint is never dropped and added again under another name; see @ref supportsRename()
        */
        list<string> getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            return ();
        }

        #! returns @ref False "False": Firebird cannot rename a constraint
        /** Table alignment takes a unique constraint with the columns of the template's under another name for the
            template's, and keeps it under its name in the database; see
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractConstraint::supportsRename() "AbstractConstraint::supportsRename()"

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2
        */
        bool supportsRename() {
            return False;
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to create the constraint in the database
        string getCreateSql(string name, string table_name, *hash opt) {
            # the columns are given by their SQL names; the names of the columns were given as they are, so a reserved
            # word in a unique constraint was not quoted
            return sprintf("alter table %s add constraint %s unique (%s)", table_name,
                firebird_get_sql_identifier(name), (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()));
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to drop the constraint from the database
        string getDropSql(string table_name) {
            return sprintf("alter table %s drop constraint %s", table_name, firebird_get_sql_identifier(name));
        }

        #! returns the SQL that disables and re-enables the foreign constraints that reference the constraint
        /** The unique constraint that a unique index of a table description has is the index, so recreating the
            index recreates the constraint: the constraint is not dropped and added again around the recreation of
            its index, which dropped a constraint that the index is not and created the index a second time; only the
            foreign constraints that reference it are disabled and re-enabled

            @param ds the datasource of the tables of the foreign constraints
            @param table_name this argument is ignored
            @param opts options for the SQL that re-enables the foreign constraints

            @return a hash with a \c disable key, giving a list of SQL strings that disable the foreign constraints
            that reference this constraint, and an \c enable key, giving a list of SQL strings that re-enable them

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2
        */
        hash<auto> getDisableReenableSql(AbstractDatasource ds, string table_name, *hash<auto> opts) {
            return getSourceConstraintDisableReenableSql(ds, opts);
        }
    }

    #! represents a Firebird-specific primary key constraint
    public class XdbcFirebirdPrimaryKey inherits SqlUtil::AbstractPrimaryKey {
        constructor() {
        }

        constructor(hash<auto> c) : AbstractPrimaryKey("PRIMARY", c) {
        }

        #! returns the column value of the given key if it exists, otherwise throws a \c KEY-ERROR exception
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
auto v = c.name;
            @endcode

            @param k the name of the key to access

            @return the value of the given key in the contained hash if it exists

            @throw KEY-ERROR the given key does not exist in the contained hash

            @note this method is called automatically when an unknown or inaccessible member name is accessed from
            outside the class

            @see @ref memberGate_methods
        */
        XdbcFirebirdColumn memberGate(string k) {
            if (!h.hasKey(k)) {
                throw "KEY-ERROR", sprintf("cannot access unknown %s %y; expecting one of: %y", getElementName(), k,
                    h.keys());
            }
            return h{k};
        }

        string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opts) {
            return sprintf("alter table %s add primary key (%s)", table_name,
                (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()));
        }

        list<string> getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            throw "UNSUPPORTED";
        }

        #! returns @ref False "False" since primary key constraints in Firebird have no name
        bool supportsName() {
            return False;
        }
    }

    #! represents a Firebird-specific trigger
    public class XdbcFirebirdTrigger inherits SqlUtil::AbstractTrigger {
        #! creates the object and sets its name and the trigger source
        constructor(string n, string n_src) : AbstractTrigger(n, n_src) {
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to create the trigger in the database
        softlist getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            return sprintf("create trigger %s %s", firebird_get_sql_identifier(name), src);
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to drop the trigger from the database
        softlist getDropSql(string table_name) {
            return sprintf("drop trigger %s", firebird_get_sql_identifier(name));
        }

        #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current object, @ref False "False" if not
        private bool equalImpl(AbstractFunctionBase t) {
            return True;
        }

        #! returns the SQL that renames the trigger: Firebird cannot rename a trigger, so it is dropped and created under the
        #! new name
        /** A trigger is only its definition, which is created again unchanged under the new name

            @param table_name the name of the table
            @param new_name the new name of the trigger

            @return the SQL statements that drop the trigger and create it under the new name
        */
        softlist getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            return (getDropSql(table_name)[0], sprintf("create trigger %s %s", firebird_get_sql_identifier(new_name),
                src));
        }
    }

    #! class for XDBC Firebird sequences
    /** A Firebird sequence has a starting value and an increment, and no minimum, maximum, cycle option or cache
        (see @ref xdbcfirebird_sequences)
    */
    public class XdbcFirebirdSequence inherits SqlUtil::AbstractSequence {
        private {
            # the parameters of the description that Firebird sequences do not have, refused by getCreateSql()
            list<string> unsupported = ();
        }

        #! creates the object from the arguments
        /** @param n_name the name of the sequence
            @param n_start the starting value
            @param n_increment the increment
            @param n_end the maximum value; Firebird sequences have none, so getCreateSql() refuses a sequence with
            a maximum
        */
        constructor(string n_name, number n_start = 1, number n_increment = 1, *softnumber n_end)
                : AbstractSequence(n_name, n_start, n_increment, n_end) {
        }

        #! records the parameters of the description that Firebird sequences do not have
        /** getCreateSql() refuses a sequence whose description has a minimum, a maximum, the cycle option or a
            cache; this method does not, so that the SQL that drops such a sequence can be given

            @param desc the sequence description (see
                @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::SequenceDescriptionOptions "SequenceDescriptionOptions")

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2
        */
        setDescription(hash<auto> desc) {
            unsupported = XdbcFirebirdSequence::getUnsupportedParameters(desc);
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to create the sequence in the database
        /** The starting value and the increment are given when they are not the defaults (1), so the SQL that
            creates a sequence with the defaults is as before

            @par Example:
            @code{.py}
# "create sequence seq_orders start with 1000 increment by 10"
string sql = new XdbcFirebirdSequence("seq_orders", 1000, 10).getCreateSql();
            @endcode

            @throw SEQUENCE-ERROR the sequence has a minimum, a maximum, the cycle option or a cache, which Firebird
            sequences do not have

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2 the starting value and the increment are given; before, a sequence was
            always created with the defaults
        */
        string getCreateSql(*hash opt) {
            list<string> params = unsupported;
            if (exists max && !inlist("max", params) && !inlist("end", params)) {
                params += "max";
            }
            XdbcFirebirdSequence::checkParameters(name, params);
            string sql = sprintf("create sequence %s", firebird_get_sql_identifier(name));
            if (start != 1) {
                sql += sprintf(" start with %d", start);
            }
            if (increment != 1) {
                sql += sprintf(" increment by %d", increment);
            }
            return sql;
        }

        #! returns the SQL that changes the increment of the sequence in the database, keeping its current value
        /** Firebird keeps the increment of a sequence, which <tt>NEXT VALUE FOR</tt> uses, and changes it with
            <tt>ALTER SEQUENCE ... INCREMENT BY</tt>; the starting value is never changed, as that would restart the
            sequence

            @param desired the sequence description (see
                @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::SequenceDescriptionOptions "SequenceDescriptionOptions")
            @param opt this argument is ignored

            @return the SQL that changes the increment, or NOTHING if the increment is not given or is not changed

            @throw SEQUENCE-ERROR the description has a minimum, a maximum, the cycle option or a cache, which
            Firebird sequences do not have

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2
        */
        *softlist<string> getAlterSql(hash<auto> desired, *hash<auto> opt) {
            XdbcFirebirdSequence::checkParameters(name, XdbcFirebirdSequence::getUnsupportedParameters(desired));
            if (exists desired.increment && desired.increment != increment) {
                if (!desired.increment) {
                    throw "SEQUENCE-ERROR", sprintf("sequence %y: increment cannot be zero", name);
                }
                return sprintf("alter sequence %s increment by %d", firebird_get_sql_identifier(name),
                    desired.increment);
            }
        }

        # returns the parameters of a description that Firebird sequences do not have; a cache of 0 is no cache, as
        # Firebird sequences have, and the cycle option False is no cycle
        private static list<string> getUnsupportedParameters(hash<auto> desc) {
            list<string> rv = ();
            foreach string key in (("min", "max", "end")) {
                if (exists desc{key}) {
                    rv += key;
                }
            }
            if (desc.cycle) {
                rv += "cycle";
            }
            if (desc.cache) {
                rv += "cache";
            }
            return rv;
        }

        private static checkParameters(string name, list<string> params) {
            if (params) {
                throw "SEQUENCE-ERROR", sprintf("sequence %y: Firebird sequences have only a starting value and an "
                    "increment, so the sequence cannot have %s %y", name, params.size() == 1 ? "the parameter"
                    : "the parameters", params);
            }
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to drop the sequence from the database
        /** @param opt this argument is ignored

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2 the name of the sequence is quoted if it is not an ordinary name
        */
        string getDropSql(*hash<auto> opt) {
            return sprintf("drop sequence %s", firebird_get_sql_identifier(name));
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to rename the sequence in the database
        /** The object is renamed in place with this call
            @param new_name the new name of the object
            @param opt this option is ignored
         */
        softlist getRenameSql(string new_name, *hash opt) {
            throw "UNSUPPORTED";
        }
    }

    #! represents a Firebird view
    public class XdbcFirebirdView inherits SqlUtil::AbstractView {
        #! creates the object from the arguments
        constructor(string name, string src) : AbstractView(name, src) {
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to create the view in the database
        string getCreateSql(*hash<auto> opt) {
            # TODO/FIXME: check options
            return sprintf("create view %s as %s", firebird_get_sql_identifier(name), src);
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to drop the view from the database
        /** @param opt this argument is ignored

            @since XdbcFirebirdSqlUtilBase 0.2 the name of the view is quoted if it is not an ordinary name
        */
        string getDropSql(*hash<auto> opt) {
            return sprintf("drop view %s", firebird_get_sql_identifier(name));
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to rename the view in the database
        /** @param new_name the new name of the object
            @param opt this argument is ignored
         */
        softlist getRenameSql(string new_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            throw "UNSUPPORTED";
        }
    }

